import{_ as t,o,c as n,a3 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"2.3 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"2.3 阻塞端与流通端：你控控我，我控控你","archivedAt":"2026-10-31","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=Bk5lLqDsnmVVaFwefNgViT","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-Bk5lLqDsnmVVaFwefNgViT.md"}'),r={name:"2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you.md"};function a(l,e,s,c,h,d){return o(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_2-3-阻塞端与流通端-你控控我-我控控你" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="_2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you" tabindex="-1">2.3 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-3-blocking-and-flowing-branches-you-control-me-i-control-you" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2\\.3 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>Bk5lLqDsnmVVaFwefNgViT</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=Bk5lLqDsnmVVaFwefNgViT" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>From the perspective of control, blocking is a <strong>means of control</strong>.</p><p><span id="source-block-TK5U3YUOkwhUJ6sOHGvHps"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="分流的角度" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="splitting" tabindex="-1">Splitting <a class="header-anchor" href="#splitting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Splitting&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In the preceding discussion, when <u>one production route splits into two</u> and one becomes blocked, a <strong>control relationship</strong> (or causal relationship) arises between them.</p><p>For both types of blocking, <strong>the route <u>we intend</u> to block first is called the blocking branch</strong>—<s>not a different route that blocked because you built it incorrectly</s>—and the other the <strong>flowing branch</strong>. The blocking branch&#39;s production rate is often subject to various <strong>external constraints</strong>, changing as those constraints change. For switching-type blocking, the constraint is <u>full storage</u>; clearing or consuming the inventory restores production. For balancing-type blocking, it is <u>a blocking flow limiter or the supply of other recipe inputs</u>. Changing the limiter&#39;s rate or the other input supplies changes the blocking branch&#39;s production rate.</p><p><strong>Production on the flowing branch is determined entirely by the blocking branch</strong>: blocking diverts all or some flow to the flowing branch. When blocking clears, flow returns to the blocking branch and the flowing branch slows. The control sequence is: <strong>external constraints first affect blocking-branch production, which then affects flowing-branch production</strong>. Thus:</p><p><span class="mark-purple"><strong>External constraints → blocking branch → flowing branch</strong></span></p><p>The blocking branch therefore often determines upstream production from downstream blocked-buffer space / production rate, while the flowing branch produces from whatever materials upstream gives it. Without incoming materials, it is free of work.</p><p><span class="mark-blue">Put as directly as possible: the blocking branch is locally full, the flowing branch locally empty. Full storage can produce only as much as leaves; empty storage produces as much as enters.</span></p><blockquote><p>This matters for the later gas/fluid feedback adaptive designs: the flowing branch cannot use output-driven control, and the blocking branch cannot use feedforward control.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>In some discussions, people also call the flowing branch the terminal branch.</p></blockquote><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-e0260d52f80e9397.q90-f9d858c02b714c06.webp" alt="23 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You"></p><div class="logistics-image-caption" data-source-block="77PM06DCTK8TypTl2Lrb7b"><p>The simplest example: the Fitting Unit is on the blocking branch, and the Moulding Unit is on the flowing branch.</p></div><p><span class="mark-blue"><strong>In the simplest example above, the Fitting Unit branch is the blocking branch and the Moulding Unit branch the flowing branch.</strong></span><span class="mark-blue"> The blocking branch makes Ferrium Parts at the Fitting Unit&#39;s maximum rate until storage fills, but keeps “listening” to their stored quantity and restarts immediately when any are removed. The flowing Moulding Unit makes Ferrium Bottles from whatever Ferrium it receives.</span></p><blockquote><p>This of course assumes unchanged total material flow entering the flowing + blocking branches.</p></blockquote><p><strong>With a priority scheme, <u>connect the priority output to blocking-branch production and the secondary output to flowing-branch production</u>. With a splitter scheme, ensure <u>sufficient flow to the blocking branch</u>, or the production objective cannot be met (see below).</strong></p><p>The flowing branch is generally sized to accept all materials diverted by blocking, preventing input supply from blocking. It can also block, diverting materials to a third route. Relative to that third route it then becomes a blocking branch, with the third route the new flowing branch. Control becomes external constraints → route 1 → route 2 → route 3.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Blocking branch</th><th>Flowing branch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Relative production priority</td><td>High: satisfied first</td><td>Low: does not mean no production</td></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>Blocked</td><td>Flowing</td></tr><tr><td>Machine buffer</td><td>Full</td><td>Empty</td></tr><tr><td>Production constraint</td><td>Output-buffer blocking, shortage of other inputs, etc.</td><td>Input starvation</td></tr><tr><td>Flow notation (section 2.5)</td><td>XX-</td><td>YY+</td></tr><tr><td>Causality</td><td>Production rate restricted by external constraints</td><td>Production rate restricted to flow left over from the blocking branch</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>In section 2.1&#39;s example, Ferrium Ore remaining in storage can be regarded as the third route.</p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-CAZ0d2JyieISTjVOQ2jJ3Q"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="汇流的角度" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="merging" tabindex="-1">Merging <a class="header-anchor" href="#merging" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Merging&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Convergers, as the dual case, behave exactly oppositely. The blocked upstream branch&#39;s flow depends on the flowing branch&#39;s flow: in the figure below, more Cuprium Ore means less Ferrium Ore flow.</p><p>Again call them the blocking and flowing branches. If the flowing branch is externally constrained, control is:</p><p><span class="mark-purple"><strong>External constraints → flowing branch → blocking branch</strong></span></p><p>The control direction changes at the converger. A full belt plays the same role for a converger as an empty belt does for a splitter: the former can always obtain an item from a full belt; the latter can always output into an empty belt.</p><p>The flow notation (section 2.5) also changes: the blocking branch is XX+ and the flowing branch YY-.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-86409d775be21737.q90-b3990af80072a4f0.webp" alt="23 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You"></p><p>Blocking-flow theory studies this extensively too (link pending). Machines in blocking-based production can behave similarly. Below, blocked Originium Powder flow depends on Xircon flow, naturally because the supplied Xircon determines how much powder can be consumed. Notice that this control need not use a machine at all: a converger suffices, provided Originium Powder is blocked.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-528b1cf648896905.q90-eb50c687009a94ef.webp" alt="23 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You"></p><p>Two different materials are not required. Below, downstream accepts at most 18/m Cuprium Bottles. The blocked upstream Cuprium branch depends on the flowing Cuprium branch: the 10/m flowing branch can always enter the Moulding Unit, and total consumption is 36/m, leaving only 26/m for the blocked branch. This commonly explains an output port below full rate.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-45b149f8f087d0b8.q90-ea1c7e87c13b21c0.webp" alt="23 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You"></p><p>Machines are not entirely equivalent to convergers, however. In the next example, 2/3 of Ferrium Ore flow goes to a Fitting Unit and 1/3 to a Moulding Unit, eventually combining in a Packaging Unit into Yazhen Syringe [C]. The Fitting Unit blocks intermittently. In the splitter/converger graph below, 2/3 goes to the upper half and 1/3 to the lower half, eventually merging, but no blocking actually occurs.</p><p>So where is the difference? Put those clever little brains to work.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-ccde73abc1fc2989.q90-963d3167ae95758f.webp" alt="23 Blocking and Flowing Branches: You Control Me, I Control You"></p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/2-2-two-branches-bloom-switching-type-and-balancing-type-blocking">2.2 Two Branches Bloom: Switching-Type and Balancing-Type Blocking</a></p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/2-4-faster-than-light-upstream-propagation-of-blocking-signals">2.4 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals</a></p><blockquote><p>Translation note: The final example uses the author’s medicine shorthand, identified as Yazhen Syringe [C] by its recipe context.</p></blockquote></div>',1)])])}const u=t(r,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
